# Learning goals - the checks to run before a goal reaches Sathya

One screen. The rules, the example and the corrections they came from are in `references/rules.md` in this folder - read that once, then run this list on every line labeled "Learning goal" or its equivalent. Every line traces to a section there, named in parentheses. When a rule changes in `references/rules.md`, its test changes here in the same edit (and the other way round).

## On every goal

- It is one sentence carrying the what (the action or outcome the student owns) and the why (the payoff to the student). (The discipline)
- The payoff is a capability the student ends up holding, not someone else's reaction - no recruiter noticing them, no instructor willing to vouch. (The discipline)
- The goal states the student's outcome, not the activity's mechanics or where the activity sits in our process. (The discipline)
- No internal vocabulary (niche-drift, spine document, "Layer 3"), no assignment named by version, no pipeline step, no week number. (The discipline)
- Every clause changes what the student understands or why they would bother; none survives only because it is true. (The discipline)
- Where the payoff is a recognizable, transferable skill, the goal names it ("Behavioral interview practice: …"); otherwise it gives a plain, concrete payoff. (Name the skill, when there is one)
- An activity with two goals has two distinct payoffs. (Name the skill, when there is one)
- The payoff is the real one, not an inflated one ("puts you in the path of good opportunities", not "lands you the job"). (Keep it credible)
- The payoff is framed as something to move toward; no FOMO, dread or warning as the hook. (Inspire, don't scare)
- It names what the student walks away with, then stops - no list of parts, steps or artifacts. (Name the outcome, not the inventory)

## Before it reaches him

- Read cold, as a student who has never seen the program: both what they are doing and why they would want to are clear, and no word needs the rest of the course to decode. (The test)
